3. Problem Statement 
Current situation: Mister Auto & Turbo currently manages customer intake, job cards, quotations and repair tracking using largely manual, paper-based methods, and has no online presence beyond wordof-mouth referrals. 
Problem: Customers have no way to check a vehicle's repair progress without phoning the workshop directly, there is no fixed or published pricing for common services, and there is no clear breakdown of cost differences when a customer supplies their own part instead of the business sourcing it. This leads to repeated interruptions, pricing disputes and difficulty attracting new customers without an online presence. 
Affected users: Customers experience poor visibility into repair progress and pricing uncertainty; reception and mechanics are repeatedly interrupted by status-check calls and price negotiations; the manager/owner lacks a way to reach new customers online and lacks centralised business records. 
Consequences: Frequent phone interruptions during active repair work, recurring disputes over pricing and discounts, missed opportunities to attract new customers, and no centralised repair history for returning customers. 
Need for improvement: A digital system is needed to give customers visibility into repair status without phoning in, present clear itemised pricing (including customer-supplied part discounts), and centralise vehicle and customer records. 
 
 
4. Project Process Aim and Objectives 
Aim: To design and develop an integrated Vehicle Service Management System that digitises the vehicle servicing lifecycle for Mister Auto & Turbo, improving record-keeping, communication and operational visibility. 
Objectives: 
•Replace paper-based job cards with a digital job card and status-tracking system. 
•Provide customers with visibility of their vehicle's status via a mobile application. 
•Provide reception, mechanics and management with role-appropriate dashboards for their tasks. 
•Centralise customer, vehicle, quotation and payment records in a shared backend. 
•Deliver a working prototype that has been validated against the stakeholder's actual process by the end of Phase  
