9. Current Process and Diagram 

Based on stakeholder engagement, the current process typically follows these steps: 
•Customer brings the vehicle in, describing the issue or requesting a scheduled service. 
•A staff member or mechanic performs an initial inspection. 
•A job card is created, usually on paper, recording the vehicle and reported issue. 
•The mechanic diagnoses the fault and prepares a quotation for parts and labour. 
•The customer is contacted to approve the quotation before work proceeds. 
•The repair is carried out. 
•A quality check confirms the work is complete and correct. 
•Payment is collected. 
•The customer collects the vehicle. 
•Reviews from Customers
 
9.1 Problems with the Current Process 
•Job cards and records are paper-based and can be lost or misplaced. 
•No way for customers to track a vehicle's repair progress remotely customers call in regularly to check status, this interrupts workshop staff. 
•No fixed, published price list for common services and parts, which leads to friction with customers negotiating for discounts on the spot. 
•No clear breakdown of cost differences when a customer supplies their own part versus the business supplying it (e.g. customer-supplied battery vs. shop-supplied battery), leading to disputes over pricing. 
•The business relies almost entirely on word-of-mouth referrals; until recently it had no social media presence and still has no website, making it difficult to reach new customers. 
•Repair history is not centrally stored, making it hard to reference past work on a vehicle. 



   
                           10. Product Perspective


The Vehicle Service Management System (VSMS) is a new standalone software solution developed for Mister Auto & Turbo to digitise and streamline the vehicle servicing process. The system is intended to replace the current paper-based methods used for job cards, repair tracking, quotations, and customer communication.
The product will provide a centralized platform for managing customer, vehicle, repair, quotation, and payment information. It will serve as a bridge between customers and workshop staff by improving transparency and communication throughout the repair lifecycle.
Unlike the current process, where customers must contact the workshop directly for updates, the proposed system will allow customers to track the status of their vehicles in real time through a mobile or web application. Staff members will be able to update repair progress digitally, while management will be able to monitor operations through administrative dashboards and reporting features.

