2. System Overview
The Mr Auto & Turbo Workshop Management System is designed as one integrated system built from three connected components: a Flutter mobile application, an ASP.NET web application, and a shared Supabase backend.
The Flutter mobile application serves both customers and workshop staff. Customers use it to track their vehicle's repair status in real time, view notifications, and manage payments. Staff use the same application, under a different role-based view, to check job status on the go and receive job-assignment notifications. Sign-in is handled through a phone-number and OTP (one-time PIN) flow, matching the way the business already communicates with customers over SMS/WhatsApp.
The ASP.NET web application is the primary workstation tool for the business, providing three role-based views: an Administrator/Manager view with full access to all modules including payments and reporting; a Receptionist view scoped to front-desk functions such as customer registration, vehicle check-in, appointments, and collections (with payment processing restricted, since only management handles payments); and a Mechanic view scoped to assigned jobs, inspection checklists, and parts requests.
The Supabase backend provides the shared PostgreSQL database, authentication, and real-time data services that both the Flutter and ASP.NET applications read from and write to. This ensures that a status update made by a mechanic on the web application is reflected immediately in the customer's mobile app, and that a booking made by a receptionist is immediately visible to the assigned mechanic - the three components operate as a single, real-time system rather than separate, disconnected tools.
